Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "Strength dipped across your major lifts this week — recovery and consistency are the priority right now.",
  "summary": "You put in a solid 28 sets this week with nearly 10,800 kg of total tonnage, which is a respectable training load for your profile. However, the strength index tells a clear story: chest, back, and legs all declined compared to last period, while shoulders, biceps, and triceps held steady. This pattern — where the largest, most demanding muscle groups regress while smaller ones maintain — is a classic signal of accumulated fatigue or insufficient recovery between sessions. The good news is that your volume split is fairly well-balanced across muscle groups, and your overall tonnage per bodyweight of 149 is solid for a 28-year-old male at your size. The priority for next period is protecting and rebuilding that strength in your big three movement patterns before pushing the load higher again.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "status": "regressing",
    "notes": "Chest dropped from 106 to 101, back from 104 to 100, and legs from 108 to 102 — all three primary muscle groups declined meaningfully this period. Shoulders, biceps, and triceps remained flat at 103, 102, and 104 respectively, meaning no group improved. This is a regression period, not a progression one. Last period's note highlighted steady progress and advised watching recovery — that recovery concern appears to have materialized. No lift is currently trending upward, which means the progressive overload stimulus for muscle growth has stalled."
  },
  "muscleImbalances": [
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Biceps",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Biceps account for only 4% of total volume split, the lowest of any group tracked. While direct biceps work is often complemented by back training, this share is notably low compared to triceps at 8.4% and may lead to an elbow-flexor deficit over time if not addressed."
    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Legs",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Legs represent 22.4% of volume but carry the largest strength index drop this period (108 to 102), suggesting they may be underrecovering relative to the stimulus being applied. The tonnage is lower than chest and back despite being the largest muscle group, which could also indicate the loads used are not matching their capacity."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": true,
  "deloadReason": "All three primary muscle groups regressed in strength index this period, and none of the six tracked groups improved. This broad-based decline — especially in the most taxing lifts (chest, back, legs) — strongly suggests accumulated systemic fatigue. Last period's recommendation flagged the need to watch recovery, and this period's data confirms that warning was warranted. A short deload of reduced volume and intensity will allow the body to consolidate the adaptations built over recent periods and set the stage for renewed progress toward the muscle-building goal.",
  "actionItems": [
    "Perform a deload this coming period by reducing your working sets by roughly 40 to 50 percent and dropping load to around 60 to 70 percent of your usual weights — keep the movement patterns the same but prioritize feeling recovered, not challenged.",
    "Prioritize sleep and nutrition during the deload week, as muscle growth happens during recovery; aim for consistent protein intake across the day to support tissue repair after this high-tonnage block.",
    "After the deload, reintroduce chest, back, and leg work conservatively — start at weights slightly below where you were last period and rebuild with small, reliable jumps each session rather than jumping straight back to peak loads.",
    "Add one additional direct biceps set next period to bring that muscle group closer in volume proportion to triceps, helping prevent a longer-term pulling-pushing imbalance at the elbow."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "Strength index regressed across all three primary muscle groups (chest, back, legs) this period following a high-tonnage week, signaling accumulated fatigue; a deload was recommended and the user should return to progressive loading conservatively the following period."
}
